## Rules

**A snapshot.** `session.snapshot()` returns the state as of the end of the last call
that finished. It is captured when each queued call ends, and starts as the parsed
`resume`, or the fresh state, so that reading it while calls are queued or right after a
resume is well defined. A UID appears in `flags` under exactly one flag list; the same
flags in another order are another entry, and a message with no flags is in `uids` only.
A session in the logout state snapshots as `logged-out`, with `buffer` empty.

**No password is saved.** While not authenticated, a snapshot's `buffer` is `""` and
`granted` is 0, always. TCP splits a LOGIN anywhere, so a partial command can hold a
password; it stays in memory. If the object is evicted in the middle of a login, the
client's next bytes are read as a new command. That takes ten seconds of silence during
a login, which a real client does not do, and the worst case is a failed login and a
retry. A fatal line clears `buffer`. The test splits a login at every byte, plain and as
literals, and looks in every snapshot. Once authenticated, a partial command can hold
only IMAP data.

**Resuming.** `createImapSession({ store, resume })` parses `resume` with `snapshotSchema`
and throws its `ZodError` when it fails. For `selected` and `idling` it calls
`store.held(account, mailbox, unranges(uids))`, once, as the first queued call, and
builds the view from it: a message's `uid`, `internalDate` and `size` come from `held`,
and its `flags` from the snapshot, never from the store, because the flags the client
was told are what the next sync compares against. It sends nothing to the client: no
greeting and no `+ idling`. A `held` that returns a different count rejects, as a store
failure does.

The test of all this is that a resumed session cannot be told apart: **every step of
every IMAP golden, with the session discarded and resumed from its snapshot after every
step, gives the same bytes**, whole and one byte at a time. While not authenticated, the
test does not discard in the middle of a command, per the rule above.

**Bounding what is saved.** After the consume loop, a session whose `buffer` still passes
`MAX_LITERAL` plus `MAX_LINE` bytes sends `* BYE command too long`, clears it and closes.
Checking after the loop means a frame of many whole commands is answered, not refused.
This closes the IMAP form's open item on a command of many literals. A test pins two
sizes: 100,000 contiguous UIDs with no flags under 1 KiB, and 100,000 alternating `\Seen`
under 1 MiB.

**The session object.** One per connection, from `newUniqueId()`. Its name is
`ctx.id.toString()`, and the mailbox reaches it with `IMAP_SESSION.idFromString(name)`,
never `idFromName`, which would reach a different, empty object that forgets the real one.
Its storage is a SQLite table `state (k TEXT PRIMARY KEY, v BLOB)`, created on first write, with four
rows: `snapshot`, the snapshot without its buffer as JSON; `buffer`, the buffer's octets as a raw BLOB,
so binary bytes do not expand; `address`, the client address; and `account`, written on becoming
authenticated, because a `logged-out` snapshot no longer says which mailbox to tell. Saves go in one
`transactionSync`. **A save writes only what differs from what is stored**, so a client sending `NOOP`
in a loop before login writes nothing and costs no rows. Before login, more than 100 frames closes the
connection with 1000 and ends.

- `fetch(request)`: reads the client address from `X-Client-Address`, answering 400 when it is missing or
  empty, makes a `WebSocketPair`,
  `ctx.acceptWebSocket(server)`, creates the session, saves, sets an alarm 60 seconds on, sends
  `greeting()`, and answers 101.
- `webSocketMessage(ws, message)`: gets the session (below), then `receive`, then **saves, then
  sends**. A crash before the send loses a reply the client is still waiting for, so it
  reconnects, which is safe. Sending first and crashing would leave the client ahead of what is
  saved, which is not. A string message is encoded as UTF-8 first. When `closed()`, it closes with
  1000 and ends (below). On becoming authenticated, it deletes the alarm.
- Sending splits the bytes into frames of at most 1 MiB, in order.
- `alarm()`: with no snapshot stored, does nothing, since the client may have closed first. If the
  snapshot is still `not-authenticated`, closes with 1000 and ends. RFC 3501 5.4 allows this, and
  otherwise a connection that never logs in holds a bridge slot forever.
- `changed()`: when `ctx.getWebSockets()` is empty, or no snapshot is stored, closes any socket
  and returns `false`. Otherwise gets the session, calls its `changed`, saves, sends any bytes,
  returns `true`.
- `webSocketClose` and `webSocketError`: end, and close the socket.
- **Ending**: sets an in-memory `ended` flag, reads the `account` row, calls `forget(name)` on that
  mailbox when there is one, then `storage.deleteAll()`, which also clears the alarm. This order
  matters: deleting first loses the account, and a mailbox with a dead name keeps waking every five
  minutes. `deleteAll` deallocates the object; deleting rows would leave a database behind per
  connection. A `forget` that rejects is logged and the state is still deleted, since a tell's `false`
  cleans the name. A call finishing after `ended` saves and sends nothing.
- Getting the session: the one in memory, or a new one with `resume` from the stored snapshot and
  buffer. Nothing else in the object depends on being in memory.
- A store failure rejects that call and every later one (IMAP form). So does a save that fails,
  for instance past the 2 MB row limit. The object closes with 1011 and ends. The client
  reconnects and is told the truth.

Every store call goes to the mailbox stub for the account, so the session object holds no mail.
`login` goes to `d1Logins` with the stored client address.

**The mailbox object.** One per mailbox, named by mailbox id through `idFromName`, which is also
the session's `account`; `ctx.id.name` gives it the mailbox id. Its SQLite holds:

```sql
CREATE TABLE messages (
  uid INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
  delivery TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,
  blob TEXT NOT NULL,
  message_id TEXT,
  internal_date TEXT NOT NULL,
  size INTEGER NOT NULL,
  flags TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '[]',
  expunged INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 CHECK (expunged IN (0, 1))
);
CREATE INDEX messages_message_id ON messages (message_id);
-- A delivery that gave no message, and why, so it is not read again and can be found.
CREATE TABLE skipped (
  delivery TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
  reason TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (reason IN ('held-message-id', 'no-blob', 'unreadable')),
  message_id TEXT
);
CREATE TABLE meta (k TEXT PRIMARY KEY, v TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE sessions (name TEXT PRIMARY KEY);
```

**Pulling.** A delivery's id is a UUIDv7 of its receipt time, taken before its bytes are stored, so
id order is not commit order: a 25 MiB message can commit after a later, smaller one. A cursor at the
newest id would lose it for good. So the cursor stands ten minutes behind the newest id seen: the
48-bit millisecond prefix of a UUIDv7, lowered by 600,000, written `xxxxxxxx-xxxx`, dashed like the ids it is
compared with, because a `-` sorts below any hex digit. It only rises: a row read behind it does not lower it. `pull()` reads `SELECT id,
blob, received_at FROM deliveries WHERE mailbox = ? AND id > ? ORDER BY id LIMIT 100`, paging by the
last id read, and skips any row whose `delivery` is already in `messages` or `skipped`, before reading
R2. For each new row it reads the blob and takes the Message-ID with `readMessage` from
`@rafters/mail/read`.

- A blob that is gone goes in `skipped` as `no-blob`, with a logged line, because the blob is written
  before the record and this means damage; one bad row must not stop every later pull.
- If a row, expunged or not, already has that Message-ID, the delivery goes in `skipped` as
  `held-message-id`, with a logged line. This follows the receiving form's "shown to the person once",
  and it can hide different mail: a sender reusing a Message-ID, or someone sending first with an id
  they guessed. The bytes stay and the `skipped` row says so.
- A Message-ID over 998 octets counts as none, since a row holds at most 2 MB and a stranger can send
  one that size in legal folded lines.
- Otherwise `INSERT OR IGNORE` adds the message. A message with no Message-ID is always added. A skipped
  insert can leave a gap in UIDs, which RFC 3501 allows.
- Any failure reading or inserting one row writes a `skipped` row as `unreadable`, with a logged line, so one
  message from a stranger cannot stop every later pull.

Each row's insert or skip, the cursor and `tell-owed` are one `transactionSync`.

Pulls are single-flight: one runs at a time, and a pull asked for during one runs once more after it,
so a ping's row is not missed. **Whenever a pull adds a row, from any caller**, every registered session
is told. If only a ping could announce, an iPhone's `SELECT` that pulled the row first would leave the
Mac in IDLE unaware.

**Telling.** **A pull commits and returns before it tells, and nothing ever waits for a tell.** `open`, `setFlags`,
`expunge`, `ping` and `alarm` return without it, because a session may be waiting on them: a session's `receive`
waits on `open`, `open` on the pull, the pull on the tell, the tell on that session's `changed()`, and `changed()` sits
in the session's queue behind `receive`. Waiting there hangs the mailbox for good, and every ping with it (research:
observed on local workerd). The tell runs in `ctx.waitUntil`.

`tell()` calls `changed()` on every registered session with `Promise.allSettled`, each bounded by 10 seconds, passing which
counts as a rejection. It is single-flight with one more pass, so a burst of changes makes few calls. `false` removes the name
from `sessions`; a rejection leaves it, with a logged line.

A commit that has not been told must not stay untold. Any commit that changes what a session should hear sets the `meta` row
`tell-owed` in its own transaction, and a pass with no rejection clears it. `alarm()` tells when it is set. A session's own changes come back to it as a
`changed()` with nothing to report, since its view already holds them, so no caller is excluded.

- `open(mailbox, session)`: `mailboxes` is `["INBOX"]`, and any other name is `null`. Registers the session,
  pulls, and returns the rows not expunged in UID order, and `uidNext` as
  `sqlite_sequence.seq + 1`, or 1 with no row there, and `uidValidity` from the `meta` row `uid_validity`. Sets the alarm when
  none is set.
- `held(uids)`: those rows, expunged or not, passing the UIDs as one JSON parameter through
  `json_each`. **Durable Object SQLite allows 100 bound parameters and a 100 KB statement**, and
  `node:sqlite` allows far more, so the test's SQLite wrapper rejects more than 100.
- `bytes(uid)`: the blob, expunged or not. `null` when the row or blob is gone.
- `setFlags(uid, flags)`: updates the row and tells.
- `expunge(uids)`: an empty list does nothing, and every CLOSE sends one. Otherwise sets `expunged = 1` and tells.
- `forget(session)`: removes the name.
- `ping()`: pulls.
- `alarm()`: **first**, while any session is registered, sets another alarm in 300 seconds; then pulls in a `try` with a
  logged line, and tells when `tell-owed` is set. Setting it first means a D1 outage cannot end the chain: Cloudflare's retries
  stop after six, and then the alarm is gone. So a lost ping delays mail by minutes, not forever.

`uid_validity` is set once, when the tables are created, from the current time in seconds. RFC 3501 2.3.1.1 requires a greater
value if UIDs ever fail to persist, and D1's deliveries outlive this SQLite, so a rebuilt object must not reuse the old one.

**Logins.** `0003_imap.sql`:

```sql
-- NIST SP 800-132: the builder quotes its sentence on salts here.
CREATE TABLE imap_logins (
  user TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
  mailbox TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES mailboxes(id),
  salt TEXT NOT NULL,
  hash TEXT NOT NULL
);
-- Keyed by "user:<sha256 of user>:<address>" and by "address:<address>".
CREATE TABLE imap_login_failures (
  key TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
  count INTEGER NOT NULL,
  window_start TEXT NOT NULL
);
```

`hash` is PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA-256 at 100,000 iterations over a 16-byte salt, as lowercase hex, and `salt` is
hex. `hashPassword` hashes the octets of a binary string, because that is what the session hands over.
`examples/imap/add-login.ts` passes both `user` and the password through `binary(new TextEncoder().encode(...))`,
so non-ASCII ones match. An address key is the address, with an IPv6 address cut to its /64 and `::ffff:a.b.c.d`
mapped to IPv4, so one routed /64 is one client. `verify(user, password, address, now)`:

0. A `user` over 320 octets returns `null` without touching D1 or deriving. Otherwise a stranger could store a
   1 MiB key per guess in the database receiving writes to.

1. Reads both failure rows. When the pair has 10 or more, or the address 30 or more, inside 900 seconds of
   `window_start`, it returns `null` without deriving. This holds for any user, known or not, so it says
   nothing about who exists. Keying by address means a stranger cannot lock the owner out from elsewhere.
2. Reads the login row. With none it still derives once against a fixed salt, and returns `null` after step
   4's counting, so a missing user takes as long as a wrong password and counts the same.
3. Derives and compares in constant time. A match deletes the pair's row and returns the mailbox.
4. A mismatch adds to both rows with one upsert each, starting a new window when the old one has passed, and
   returns `null`. It also deletes rows whose window has passed.

**The Worker.** `imapWorker()` returns `{ fetch }`. Checked in this order: a path other than `/imap` answers
404; a wrong, missing or unset `BRIDGE_SECRET`, compared through an HMAC with `crypto.subtle`, answers 401;
no `Upgrade: websocket` answers 426; otherwise it goes to a new session object. An empty secret authorizes
nothing. It is a second Worker from the receiving one, since Email Routing lists only a Worker with `email`
alone.

**Receiving.** `Bindings` gains an optional `IMAP_MAILBOX`. When it is bound, after an accept the Worker calls
`ping()` on each mailbox in `verdict.mailboxes`, in a `try` with a logged line: the message is already stored,
so a failed ping must not fail the delivery, and the alarm catches it. When it is not bound nothing changes.
The binding reaches the objects by `script_name`. Another receiving provider's mail arrives by alarm, up to five
minutes late, until its adapter calls `ping`; so this does not meet the intent's switching proposal until then.

**The bridge.** It listens on `[::]:PORT`, default 1143, for what Fly's `tls` and `proxy_proto` handlers forward: a
PROXY v1 line, then plain octets. It reads the line, at most 107 octets, and takes the client address, cut to /64 for
IPv6 as Logins says. A connection with no complete line within 5 seconds closes, and it counts against `MAX_CONNECTIONS`
meanwhile. It then opens one WebSocket to `TARGET_URL` with `Authorization: Bearer <BRIDGE_SECRET>` and `X-Client-Address`,
and copies octets as binary frames both ways, in frames of at most 1 MiB, with `max_frame_size` and
`max_message_size` set to allow them. Either side closing closes both. A text frame from the Worker closes both,
since the Worker never sends one. It sends a WebSocket ping every 30 seconds, because a mail app in IDLE says
nothing and something on the way to Cloudflare will otherwise drop the connection; Cloudflare's docs say ping and
pong do not interrupt hibernation. It logs one line per connection with no octets. A connection that cannot reach
the Worker closes at once, with a 10-second bound on connecting. `X-Client-Address` is a bare IP: the /64's first address for IPv6, IPv4 for a mapped address. It holds at most `MAX_CONNECTIONS`, default 256, and `MAX_PER_ADDRESS`, default 16, and
refuses more by closing.

It reads every setting from the environment and refuses to start without `TARGET_URL` or `BRIDGE_SECRET`, or with
a `TARGET_URL` that is not `wss://`, except on loopback. Mail and passwords are in the clear on the Fly machine,
because the `tls` handler terminates there. `BRIDGE_SECRET` is set with `fly secrets set`, and with `wrangler secret put` on
the Worker, the same value.

## Tests

The writer's harness for TypeScript: every test is a `criterion(id, fn)`, and coverage is 100%. The bridge's tests
are `cargo test` functions named for their criterion, for instance `fr_mail_037_c1`; their ids are not in
`criteria.json`, and no Rust coverage is measured: the criteria are the bar. Event criteria are recorded, not run.

- **Resuming, on the goldens.** Every step of all eight IMAP goldens, discarding and resuming after every step, whole
  and one byte at a time, with the exception above.
- **The wrong-message case.** A session sees UID 1 as message 1; the store expunges UID 1; the session resumes; `FETCH
  1 (UID)` still says UID 1, and the next `NOOP` after `changed()` tells `* 1 EXPUNGE`.
- **IDLE across a resume.** A session idling is resumed, `changed()` is called, and it tells the new message.
